---
import GrafanaSettings from "../product/GrafanaSettings.astro"
import RepairQueue from "../product/RepairQueue.astro"
import Section from "../site/Section.astro"
import Stage from "../site/Stage.astro"

const stages = ["Connect", "Import", "Repair", "Keep the links"]
---

<Section>
  <div class="flow import-flow">
    {
      stages.map((stage, index) => (
        <span class="seg">
          <span class="stage">{stage}</span>
          {index < stages.length - 1 && <span class="line solid" aria-hidden="true" />}
        </span>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <div class="cols2 steps">
    <div class="step-copy">
      <div>
        <h2 class="h3">Connect</h2>
        <p class="p">
          Admins open Settings and choose Grafana connection, then give it a Grafana URL and a service account token with
          the Viewer role on the folders to import. The test counts top-level folders and dashboards; the import form
          shows each Grafana datasource beside its match.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="h3">Import, and import again</h2>
        <p class="p">
          Choose "Import", then "All folders" and "Start import". Every dashboard comes across with its tags, variables
          and links, and every imported query runs. A nested folder path is kept as one folder titled Parent / Child. A second
          run skips dashboards unchanged in Grafana and saves a new version of those that changed; your edits stay in
          the version history.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="h3">Repair</h2>
        <p class="p">
          Panels whose queries fail or return nothing go to a repair queue the agent works through; placeholders are
          listed for you to redo by hand. Those panels are marked unverified, never hidden, and "Repair with the agent"
          rewrites their queries against the metrics that exist and saves a version you can diff.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="h3">Keep the links</h2>
        <p class="p">
          Every imported dashboard keeps its Grafana uid, so <span class="mono">{"/d/<uid>/<slug>"}</span> here
          redirects to the imported dashboard. Point the old hostname at SlashObserve and runbooks, wiki pages and alert
          links keep working.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-stage" data-in-view="rise">
      <Stage>
        <div class="step-windows">
          <GrafanaSettings />
          <RepairQueue />
        </div>
      </Stage>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .import-flow {
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .stage {
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .steps {
    align-items: start;
  }

  .step-copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 28px;
  }

  .step-copy .p {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }

  .step-stage {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .step-windows {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .import-flow {
      gap: 8px 16px;
    }

    .import-flow .line {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
